Picture sheet of an STL from all sides, and numbers for how boxy its surface is. Needs numpy only.

    python tools/solidworks/sheet.py part.stl                 # writes part.sheet.png, prints the shape numbers
    python tools/solidworks/sheet.py a.stl b.stl -o both.png  # one row per part, same scale: before and after

A model that cannot see its part cannot judge it. Run this after every step that changes the shape, open the PNG and look
at it; the report then shows what the reader would see. Eight views per part: front (from -Y), back, left (from -X),
right, top, bottom and two three-quarter views, all at one scale, lit the same way, with outline and crease lines.

The shape numbers (also `shape_stats(tris)`):
    flat_share      share of the surface area lying on planes larger than 1 % of the total area
    largest_flat    the largest such plane, as a share of the area
    sharp_mm        length of edges where the surface turns by more than 30 degrees
    sharp_per_area  sharp_mm divided by the square root of the area (does not grow with the part's size)
They say how much of a part is plates and corners. They do not say whether it looks good: that takes the picture.
A smoothed flat strap and a round bone can score alike, and faces the task requires to be flat count too.

`inside(tris, points)` says which points lie in the solid: the way to check that a clearance is clear and a boss is
solid. `--crop x0,y0,z0,x1,y1,z1` draws only the triangles inside a box, to look closely at one junction.

Run it under the cap for big meshes: python tools/pc/capped.py --mb 2048 -- python tools/solidworks/sheet.py part.stl
# Benchmark tasks, set B

Five tasks on parts the first set ([TASKS.md](TASKS.md)) did not use. Set A measured whether a session gets the right
numbers. Set B measures what it does once the numbers are right. Same rules as set A: frame X, Y, Z in mm; printed PETG as
in `swfea.PETG` (yield 45 MPa in the layer plane, 22.5 MPa in tension across layers, 13 MPa in shear between layers);
models in `cad/_wip/sw-eval/<run>/`; scripts, `REPORT.md` and `results.json` in `tools/solidworks/playbook/eval/<run>/`.
`results.json` holds one object per task with the keys named below, `"status"` and `"notes"`. Do not claim a check you did
not run.

## B1. Model a hub from a specification

A flanged hub, one solid body, axis along Z.

- Flange: Ã˜60, Z 0 to 5.
- Boss: Ã˜26, Z 5 to 25.
- Bore: Ã˜10, through, on the axis.
- Six Ã˜4.5 holes through the flange on a Ã˜44 circle, the first on +X, 60 degrees apart.
- Two Ã˜5.5 dowel holes through the flange on a Ã˜30 circle, on +Y and on -Y.
- A keyway in the bore, full length: 3 wide, centred on +X, its floor 6.4 from the axis.
- A Ã˜4 set-screw hole through the boss wall, radial, along +Y, its axis at Z 22.6, from the bore to the outside.

Save `hub.SLDPRT`, export `hub.step` and `hub.stl`.

Keys: `volume_mm3`, `hand_volume_mm3`, `mass_g`, `bbox_mm`, `bodies`, `stl_watertight`, `spec_questions` (a list of
sentences; empty if you have none).

## B2. A wall bracket: will it hold?

One solid body.

- Wall plate: X 0 to 5, Y 0 to 40, Z 0 to 60. It is screwed to a wall at X 0 through two Ã˜4.5 holes along X, at Y 20,
  Z 12 and Y 20, Z 48.
- Arm: X 5 to 65, Y 15 to 25, Z 40 to 52.
- 3 mm radius fillets where the arm meets the plate, on the arm's top and bottom (the edges along Y at X 5, Z 52 and
  X 5, Z 40).

Load: 60 N straight down (-Z), spread over the top face of the last 10 mm of the arm (X 55 to 65).

